WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:24.000
The United States Treasury Department presents the Adventures of Superman.

00:24.000 --> 00:31.000
Faster than a speeding bullet.

00:31.000 --> 00:36.000
More powerful than a locomotive.

00:36.000 --> 00:40.000
Able to leap tall buildings at a single bound.

00:40.000 --> 00:42.000
Look, up in the sky, it's a bird!

00:42.000 --> 00:43.000
It's a plane!

00:43.000 --> 00:44.000
It's Superman!

00:44.000 --> 00:52.000
Yes, it's Superman, a strange visitor from another planet who came to Earth with powers and abilities far beyond those of mortal men.

00:52.000 --> 01:09.000
Superman, who can change the course of mighty rivers, bend steel in his bare hands, and who, disguised as Clark Kent, mild-mannered reporter for a great metropolitan newspaper, fights a never-ending battle for truth, justice, and the American way.

01:23.000 --> 01:26.000
Clark, aren't they the cutest puppies you ever saw?

01:26.000 --> 01:29.000
They sure are, but did you ever see a puppy that wasn't cute?

01:29.000 --> 01:31.000
I wish the shop was open so I could hold one.

01:31.000 --> 01:35.000
You've done the next best thing. You've had me standing in front of that window for the last, what, 20 minutes?

01:35.000 --> 01:36.000
Yes.

01:36.000 --> 01:37.000
Clark, look.

01:37.000 --> 01:42.000
Oh, now, Lois, do you realize this is the 18th window you stopped at?

01:42.000 --> 01:49.000
And furthermore, do you realize that according to my calculations, at this rate, it'll take me until the middle of next week to walk you home?

01:49.000 --> 01:52.000
If you're in a hurry, I'm sure I can manage alone.

01:52.000 --> 01:57.000
I'll tell you what. You look in half the windows, and I'll look in the other half. That way, it'll just take us half as long.

01:57.000 --> 02:00.000
But you don't understand. Shopping is an art.

02:00.000 --> 02:06.000
Someone else is shopping. And from the inside, too.

02:06.000 --> 02:34.000
There's an all-night drug store in the corner. I'll call the police.

02:34.000 --> 02:38.000
My partner went out the door as soon as the alarm went off.

02:38.000 --> 02:41.000
You don't have to worry about me, though. I'm not gonna run away.

02:41.000 --> 02:44.000
You mean you deliberately stayed when you could have gotten away?

02:44.000 --> 02:48.000
Well, I've never done anything like this before, Superman. I...

02:48.000 --> 02:51.000
It was all like a dream until that alarm went off and woke me up.

02:51.000 --> 02:56.000
Then I realized that I can run from the police, but I can't run from myself.

02:56.000 --> 03:00.000
It's easy to see that you're new at this sort of thing. Why did you do it?

03:00.000 --> 03:05.000
Why? That's what I keep asking myself.

03:05.000 --> 03:10.000
Sure, it was the money, but it went deeper than that.

03:10.000 --> 03:14.000
I should have learned how to save and handle money a long time ago.

03:14.000 --> 03:17.000
Then this wouldn't have happened.

03:17.000 --> 03:21.000
Well, I'm sorry it did, but at least you've learned something very important.

03:21.000 --> 03:25.000
Well, pretty bad way to learn it, don't you think?

03:25.000 --> 03:27.000
Well, here they come, Superman.

03:27.000 --> 03:31.000
Gee, I feel a little better for having someone to talk to. Thanks.

03:31.000 --> 03:34.000
It's all right, son. Now, you can do me a favor. Don't tell them I was here.

03:34.000 --> 03:36.000
Sure.

03:36.000 --> 03:39.000
I'm sorry, I'm late.

03:39.000 --> 03:41.000
I'll be right back.

04:01.000 --> 04:04.000
Never mind, Clark. I already got the story.

04:04.000 --> 04:06.000
I got a good look at the second burglar.

04:06.000 --> 04:09.000
What? You mean the one that got away? You saw him?

04:09.000 --> 04:12.000
No. He must have gone out the back way and up the other alleys I passed.

04:12.000 --> 04:14.000
We bumped right into each other.

04:14.000 --> 04:17.000
Well, I hope you don't bump into him again.

04:19.000 --> 04:22.000
Pretty nifty, isn't it, Mr. Kent? I can almost carry it in my pocket.

04:22.000 --> 04:26.000
Now, if I have any special outside assignments, I can carry my typewriter with me.

04:26.000 --> 04:29.000
That's right, Jimmy. It looks like a good practical investment to me.

04:29.000 --> 04:33.000
There it is. Eyewitness report of a burglary that didn't burgle too well.

04:33.000 --> 04:35.000
Well, congratulations, Lois.

04:35.000 --> 04:37.000
Gee, that's cute.

04:37.000 --> 04:39.000
Cute?

04:39.000 --> 04:41.000
Can't you think of some other word to call it, Miss Lane?

04:41.000 --> 04:44.000
I don't want to be known as the boy with the cute typewriter.

04:44.000 --> 04:48.000
I'm afraid it's hopeless, Jimmy. I once knew a woman who referred to the Grand Canyon as cute.

04:48.000 --> 04:52.000
Well, whatever you call it, I didn't know the chief was handing out raises.

04:52.000 --> 04:54.000
Oh, I didn't get a raise.

04:54.000 --> 04:56.000
Then I didn't know he was handing out rich uncles.

04:56.000 --> 04:59.000
In a way, I guess you could say an uncle had something to do with it.

04:59.000 --> 05:01.000
Uncle Sam.

05:01.000 --> 05:04.000
When I was in school, I used to buy treasury savings stamps every week.

05:04.000 --> 05:07.000
By the time I graduated, they turned into bonds.

05:07.000 --> 05:09.000
So you're cashing the bonds on this typewriter?

05:09.000 --> 05:11.000
Only part of them.

05:11.000 --> 05:16.000
You see, the interest I got on my investment made the bonds worth a lot more than what I put into them.

05:16.000 --> 05:19.000
Well, that's very interesting, Jimmy. And speaking of learning to save,

05:19.000 --> 05:21.000
that ties in exactly with something I heard last night.

05:21.000 --> 05:25.000
And when did you hear anything about saving last night, Mr. Kent?

05:25.000 --> 05:27.000
I was with you all the time, remember?

05:27.000 --> 05:31.000
Oh, yes, Lois. Well, this must have been some other evening when you weren't with me.

05:31.000 --> 05:34.000
Jimmy, do they still have stamp day at your old alma mater?

05:34.000 --> 05:36.000
Far as I know, every Thursday.

05:36.000 --> 05:38.000
And this is Thursday, isn't it?

05:38.000 --> 05:40.000
That gives me an idea.

05:40.000 --> 05:45.000
Why not do a feature article on how the kids help themselves and Uncle Sam by buying bonds and stamps?

05:45.000 --> 05:47.000
Cheap as I'll go along with that.

05:47.000 --> 05:49.000
Good. How about you, Lois?

05:49.000 --> 05:51.000
I'd love to, but I have a date with Inspector Henderson.

05:51.000 --> 05:55.000
I have to look through the mug books for that speedy gentleman I met in the alley last night.

05:55.000 --> 05:57.000
Well, then come on over later. P.S. number 10.

05:57.000 --> 05:59.000
Fine. Meet you there in an hour.

05:59.000 --> 06:00.000
Jim?

06:00.000 --> 06:02.000
Yes, Miss Lane?

06:02.000 --> 06:05.000
I was wondering, could I borrow your typewriter?

06:08.000 --> 06:10.000
Thank you.

06:20.000 --> 06:21.000
Good morning, boys.

06:21.000 --> 06:22.000
Good morning, Mr. Garway.

06:22.000 --> 06:25.000
I'd like you to meet Mr. Kent and Jimmy Olsen from the planet.

06:25.000 --> 06:26.000
Hi, fellas.

06:26.000 --> 06:29.000
Mr. Kent, you're the friend of Superman, aren't you?

06:29.000 --> 06:32.000
Well, we do keep in pretty close contact, yes.

06:32.000 --> 06:36.000
Boy, if Superman would come over, I bet we'd really have a stamp day.

06:36.000 --> 06:40.000
Well, I can't promise you anything, but it might be arranged.

06:42.000 --> 06:43.000
The booth looks very nice, boys.

06:43.000 --> 06:52.000
Thank you very much.

06:52.000 --> 06:53.000
Hello?

06:53.000 --> 06:59.000
Miss Lane, this is Blinky. Remember me?

06:59.000 --> 07:02.000
Blinky? Oh, of course.

07:02.000 --> 07:04.000
Why, we used to go to Sunday school picnics together.

07:04.000 --> 07:06.000
Now you're thinking of the wrong Blinky.

07:06.000 --> 07:10.000
The only Sunday school picnic I ever went to was in reform school.

07:10.000 --> 07:12.000
Then they took us all out to chase butterflies.

07:12.000 --> 07:15.000
The one I chased just happened to fly into a moving box car.

07:15.000 --> 07:16.000
So what could I do?

07:16.000 --> 07:20.000
You know, Miss Lane, I chased that butterfly all the way up to San Francisco.

07:20.000 --> 07:23.000
Well, let's not compare life histories, Blinky. What do you want?

07:23.000 --> 07:27.000
Well, I'm the guy that ran into you yesterday. You know, by the jewelry store.

07:27.000 --> 07:29.000
Then you're the...

07:29.000 --> 07:31.000
The guy they're looking for.

07:31.000 --> 07:34.000
They're bound to get me, Miss Lane, so I'm going to surrender.

07:34.000 --> 07:37.000
Well, that's fine, Blinky. You're doing the right thing.

07:37.000 --> 07:39.000
But I ain't going to surrender to the police.

07:39.000 --> 07:41.000
I'm only surrendering to you personally.

07:41.000 --> 07:43.000
Me?

07:43.000 --> 07:46.000
Sure. They'll have to give me a break if I walk in with you.

07:46.000 --> 07:51.000
Look, meet me on the corner of Chestnut and, uh, Warlow in exactly a half hour.

07:51.000 --> 07:53.000
But alone.

07:53.000 --> 07:55.000
But I'm not sure I'll recognize you.

07:55.000 --> 08:12.000
You'll recognize me all right. In a half hour.

08:12.000 --> 08:16.000
Inspector Henderson, Lois Lane. I'll be a little late for our appointment.

08:16.000 --> 08:23.000
I have a surprise to pick up for you first.

08:23.000 --> 08:26.000
Yes, Mr. Kent, we're pretty proud of our students.

08:26.000 --> 08:28.000
Stamp day is really their idea, you know.

08:28.000 --> 08:31.000
You mean they do the whole thing themselves?

08:31.000 --> 08:34.000
Take the orders, collect the money, and keep their own books?

08:34.000 --> 08:36.000
And believe me, it isn't easy.

08:36.000 --> 08:39.000
But it sure is a good feeling at the end of the day when everything comes out all right.

08:39.000 --> 08:43.000
When it comes to buying savings stamps, Jimmy, I'm afraid everything's bound to come out all right.

08:43.000 --> 08:46.000
They'll start taking orders at lunch period.

08:46.000 --> 08:49.000
Now, that's an hour and a half from now.

08:49.000 --> 08:53.000
Mr. Kent, if you could get word to Superman.

08:53.000 --> 08:55.000
Well, Mr. Garwood, I'll do the best I can.

08:55.000 --> 08:58.000
But right now, I'm very worried about Lois.

08:58.000 --> 09:00.000
She should have been here by now. May I use your phone?

09:00.000 --> 09:01.000
Certainly.

09:01.000 --> 09:10.000
Thank you.

09:10.000 --> 09:17.000
Mr. White's office, please.

09:17.000 --> 09:18.000
White speaking.

09:18.000 --> 09:21.000
Chief, this is Kent. What's happened to Lois?

09:21.000 --> 09:27.000
Kent, I spent half my life attempting to keep track of you, Lois, or young Olsen.

09:27.000 --> 09:32.000
I spent the other half regretting that I spent the first half trying to keep track of you.

09:32.000 --> 09:36.000
I'm afraid you lost me on that last curve. When did she leave?

09:36.000 --> 09:38.000
About an hour ago. She stuck her head in the door and said,

09:38.000 --> 09:42.000
Chief, I have a date with a Heitman to wrap him up and put him on ice.

09:42.000 --> 09:44.000
As usual, it didn't make much sense.

09:44.000 --> 09:46.000
I'm afraid it makes too much sense, Chief. I'll be right over.

09:46.000 --> 09:50.000
I'm sorry. I have to go.

09:50.000 --> 09:51.000
You stay here.

09:51.000 --> 09:52.000
But, but, Mr.

09:52.000 --> 10:03.000
No buts about it, Olsen. You stick around.

10:03.000 --> 10:07.000
The things I can walk into with my eyes wide open.

10:07.000 --> 10:09.000
It ain't your fault, Miss Lane.

10:09.000 --> 10:12.000
I ain't got nothing against you personally except you can put the finger on me.

10:12.000 --> 10:15.000
So can Jess Dunlap, your partner in crime.

10:15.000 --> 10:18.000
Nah, Jess was in on the job. His testimony don't mean a thing.

10:18.000 --> 10:20.000
And what's going to happen to me?

10:20.000 --> 10:24.000
I gotta get rid of you, Miss Lane.

10:24.000 --> 10:27.000
But not until I get a call from the guy that's selling the jewelry for me.

10:27.000 --> 10:33.000
And in the meantime, if anybody catches up with you, you use me as a shield, right?

10:33.000 --> 10:35.000
That's what I like, a girl with brains.

10:35.000 --> 10:37.000
If that's what you like, find another girl.

10:37.000 --> 10:40.000
I obviously don't have any brains or I wouldn't be here.

10:40.000 --> 10:44.000
Hey, I got an idea.

10:44.000 --> 10:47.000
Look, I'm going to be out of the country a long time, right?

10:47.000 --> 10:50.000
Well, I gotta keep in touch with my pals by writing letters, right?

10:50.000 --> 10:55.000
And being as how nobody can read my handwriting, you can teach me how to run this little gadget.

10:55.000 --> 10:56.000
How about it?

10:56.000 --> 11:00.000
Sure, why not?

11:00.000 --> 11:03.000
But who's going to teach those friends of yours how to read?

11:03.000 --> 11:07.000
What a sense of humor. Nothing but laughs right up to the end.

11:07.000 --> 11:09.000
Thanks for reminding me.

11:09.000 --> 11:20.000
Well, start with less...

11:20.000 --> 11:22.000
Gee, that's beautiful, Miss Lane.

11:22.000 --> 11:25.000
Every time I think of this line, I'll think of you.

11:25.000 --> 11:32.000
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.

11:32.000 --> 11:34.000
That's all I know, Kent.

11:34.000 --> 11:36.000
She phoned Henderson and said she'd be late.

11:36.000 --> 11:37.000
She hasn't shown up.

11:37.000 --> 11:42.000
Well, it's a safe guess that whoever she saw last night has lured into some kind of a trap.

11:42.000 --> 11:45.000
Well, unfortunately, that doesn't help us locate the trap.

11:45.000 --> 11:47.000
There's only one lead I can think of.

11:47.000 --> 11:50.000
I'm going down to the jail and talk to Jess Dunlop.

11:50.000 --> 11:51.000
Oh, Kent.

11:51.000 --> 11:52.000
Yes, sir.

11:52.000 --> 11:55.000
I didn't really mean all those things I said.

11:55.000 --> 12:00.000
I know you didn't, Chief.

12:00.000 --> 12:02.000
Say, I'm doing pretty good on this one, Miss Lane.

12:02.000 --> 12:05.000
Out of a hundred letters, I only made 95 mistakes.

12:05.000 --> 12:07.000
You're doing fine, Blinky.

12:07.000 --> 12:10.000
You'll make some woman a wonderful secretary.

12:10.000 --> 12:13.000
Hey, what's that you're doing?

12:13.000 --> 12:17.000
I was just going to show you how to draw pictures with a typewriter.

12:17.000 --> 12:19.000
Now, ain't that something?

12:19.000 --> 12:20.000
No.

12:20.000 --> 12:22.000
If you look too close, it doesn't look like a picture anymore.

12:22.000 --> 12:25.000
You have to hold it far away, and then the letters sort of blur together.

12:25.000 --> 12:26.000
Why don't you try one?

12:26.000 --> 12:27.000
Sure.

12:27.000 --> 12:46.000
Don't go no further.

12:46.000 --> 12:48.000
I like you real close.

12:48.000 --> 12:50.000
I was just going to test flight my airplane.

12:50.000 --> 12:52.000
You can test flight it from right over there.

12:52.000 --> 12:57.000
Anything you say, Blinky.

12:57.000 --> 12:58.000
Now look what I did.

12:58.000 --> 13:01.000
So you can make another one.

13:01.000 --> 13:02.000
Oh, thanks, Blinky.

13:02.000 --> 13:04.000
I don't feel much like playing games.

13:04.000 --> 13:26.000
Especially when you should be getting that phone call soon.

13:26.000 --> 13:27.000
Ken.

13:27.000 --> 13:28.000
Ken.

13:28.000 --> 13:30.000
Nothing to get excited about, Chief.

13:30.000 --> 13:32.000
I talked to Jess all right, and he identified the other fellow,

13:32.000 --> 13:34.000
but he doesn't know where he lives.

13:34.000 --> 13:35.000
Well, now don't worry about me.

13:35.000 --> 13:37.000
I haven't suddenly gone childish.

13:37.000 --> 13:39.000
But take a look at this.

13:39.000 --> 13:40.000
Hmm.

13:40.000 --> 13:42.000
Daily Planet urgent.

13:42.000 --> 13:43.000
Someone found it on the street and brought it in.

13:43.000 --> 13:47.000
Take a look inside.

13:47.000 --> 13:51.000
Well, if that somebody thinks this is any time for practical jokes.

13:51.000 --> 13:52.000
Wait a minute, Chief.

13:52.000 --> 13:53.000
Just a second.

13:53.000 --> 13:57.000
Look here among the X's.

13:57.000 --> 14:00.000
Prisoner, top floor, 18 Cedar Street.

14:00.000 --> 14:02.000
Ken, that's it.

14:02.000 --> 14:04.000
Well, if it is, I hope I'm time.

14:04.000 --> 14:05.000
Get me the police.

14:05.000 --> 14:06.000
I better get over there, Chief.

14:06.000 --> 14:17.000
I might be able to be of some help.

14:17.000 --> 14:28.000
Yeah.

14:28.000 --> 14:31.000
Good.

14:31.000 --> 14:33.000
That's a call I've been waiting for, Miss Lane.

14:33.000 --> 14:42.000
Everything's been taken care of except you.

14:42.000 --> 14:43.000
Mikey, don't.

14:43.000 --> 14:47.000
You just get a few years for the jewel robbery, but for this.

14:47.000 --> 14:55.000
That's the chance I got to take.

14:55.000 --> 14:56.000
No.

14:56.000 --> 14:58.000
I don't believe it.

14:58.000 --> 14:59.000
You will, Blinky.

14:59.000 --> 15:00.000
You will.

15:00.000 --> 15:04.000
You're just wasting your ammunition, Blinky.

15:04.000 --> 15:10.000
Sit down.

15:10.000 --> 15:12.000
Hey, let me out.

15:12.000 --> 15:13.000
Let me out.

15:13.000 --> 15:16.000
Another few seconds and it would have been too late, Superman.

15:16.000 --> 15:18.000
Well, it wasn't, Miss Lane, and that's what counts.

15:18.000 --> 15:19.000
What time is it?

15:19.000 --> 15:20.000
Just about 12.30.

15:20.000 --> 15:21.000
Well, I can't wait for the police.

15:21.000 --> 15:22.000
I have an appointment date.

15:22.000 --> 15:23.000
See you later.

15:23.000 --> 15:28.000
Well, you won't have to worry about riding your pals now, Blinky.

15:28.000 --> 15:31.000
Hiya, boys and gals.

15:31.000 --> 15:34.000
There can only be one Superman, of course.

15:34.000 --> 15:38.000
Did you ever think about some of the super things that you can do for yourself?

15:38.000 --> 15:43.000
Well, like saving up the money for your own vacation or for that new bike that you want

15:43.000 --> 15:44.000
to buy?

15:44.000 --> 15:45.000
Well, I don't know.

15:45.000 --> 15:46.000
I don't know.

15:46.000 --> 15:47.000
I don't know.

15:47.000 --> 15:48.000
I don't know.

15:48.000 --> 15:49.000
I don't know.

15:49.000 --> 15:54.000
I don't know.

15:54.000 --> 15:58.000
Well, all you have to do is just put away part of your allowance or your odd job money

15:58.000 --> 16:02.000
and put it in United States savings stamps at school.

16:02.000 --> 16:05.000
Those dimes, quarters, and dollars add up mighty fast, especially when you buy them

16:05.000 --> 16:07.000
every week on stamp day.

16:07.000 --> 16:12.000
Well, the first thing you know, you'll have enough for a savings bond, just like Dad buys

16:12.000 --> 16:14.000
for the payroll savings at work.

16:14.000 --> 16:16.000
And from then on, the sky is the limit.

16:16.000 --> 16:17.000
Take it from Superman.

16:17.000 --> 16:21.000
Your mom and dad will be plenty proud of you if you're learning to save regularly.

16:21.000 --> 16:23.000
And the teachers are on your team, too.

16:23.000 --> 16:27.000
They make sure of having savings stamps at school for you to buy and remind you when

16:27.000 --> 16:29.000
it is stamp day.

16:29.000 --> 16:34.000
And so, boys and girls, be super citizens and have a super future by saving regularly

16:34.000 --> 16:37.000
with United States savings stamps at school.

16:37.000 --> 16:42.000
And keep on making me and everyone else as proud of you as we are today.

16:42.000 --> 16:44.000
Bye now.

16:44.000 --> 16:47.000
Bye.

16:47.000 --> 16:50.000
Bye.

16:50.000 --> 16:53.000
Bye.

16:53.000 --> 16:56.000
Bye.

16:56.000 --> 16:59.000
Bye.

16:59.000 --> 17:03.000
Lois, it's good to see you again.

17:03.000 --> 17:05.000
All in a day's work, Clark.

17:05.000 --> 17:08.000
While you were out covering stamp day, Superman and I were cleaning up a case.

17:08.000 --> 17:12.000
It looks like Blinky's going to get jailed, Jess is going to get probation, and I'm going

17:12.000 --> 17:14.000
to get my typewriter back.

17:14.000 --> 17:18.000
Golly, Jim, it's a lucky thing for me you saved your money for that typewriter.

17:18.000 --> 17:20.000
Otherwise, I'd never gotten that note out.

17:20.000 --> 17:22.000
It turned into a happy ending for everybody.

17:22.000 --> 17:26.000
And just to make the ending a little more happy, I have a present for each of us.

17:26.000 --> 17:28.000
Cheaper stamp albums.

17:28.000 --> 17:29.000
Thank you, Mr. Kent.

17:29.000 --> 17:31.000
You have an extra one.

17:31.000 --> 17:35.000
Who's that for?

17:35.000 --> 17:37.000
Oh, just for a friend of mine.

17:37.000 --> 17:43.000
seminars.

18:43.000 --> 18:47.000
.

18:47.000 --> 18:51.000
...

18:51.000 --> 18:55.000
...

18:55.000 --> 18:59.000
...

18:59.000 --> 19:03.000
...

19:03.000 --> 19:07.000
...

19:07.000 --> 19:11.000
...

20:41.000 --> 20:47.000
.

20:47.000 --> 20:53.020
.

20:53.020 --> 20:59.020
.

20:59.020 --> 21:05.020
.

21:35.020 --> 21:40.020
.

21:40.020 --> 21:45.020
.

21:45.020 --> 21:50.020
.

21:50.020 --> 21:55.040
.

21:55.040 --> 22:00.040
.

22:00.040 --> 22:02.040
You

